HST Writer citations: text fields that keep their code through Undo, Redo, Backspace and .odt.

HST's citations and bibliography are text fieldmarks of type UNHANDLED whose code
("ADDIN HST_CITATION {...}") is a fieldmark parameter, the same shape as fields of other
programs read from .docx. Checked on the 1.0.2 Linux build, five things broke them:

- Undo/Redo dropped the code. SwHistoryTextFieldmark recorded the position, name and type of a
  text fieldmark but not its parameters, so deleting a citation and pressing Undo (or Undo then
  Redo of an insertion) brought back a field with no code. The history now keeps the parameter
  map: taken when the history is recorded and again just before the field is removed by
  ResetInDoc(), because an inserted field gets its code after makeFieldBookmark() has already
  appended the insert Undo. Date fields keep their date format the same way.
- .uno:TextFormFields and .uno:UpdateTextFormField changed the code outside Undo: Undo restored
  the old result next to the new code. Each code change now appends a
  SwUndoTextFieldmarkParameters action inside the command's existing Undo group, so one Undo
  restores both. The action finds the field by its start, which Undo's order guarantees.
- Backspace just after (or Delete just before) an UNHANDLED field removed the field and left
  its result as plain text that still looks like a citation. Such a field is now selected first,
  as in Word; the next key press deletes it whole (one Undo step that restores it with its code).
  Other fieldmark types keep the old behaviour.
- - A cursor right before an UNHANDLED field counted as inside it: typing there (or at the start
  of a paragraph that begins with a citation) and Delete there showed the read-only dialog.
  As already for form text fields, a collapsed cursor at the field start is now editable; typed
  text goes in front of the field and Delete selects it as above.
- .odt reload threw away fieldmarks in footnotes, endnotes and text frames. PopFieldmark()
  compared the import text with the field anchor's getText(), and there those are two UNO
  objects for the same text (SwXFootnote::attach() does not register itself on the footnote, so
  the anchor creates a second one). The check now falls back to createTextCursorByRange(),
  which verifies by node position that the range is in that text.

.docx export and import already kept the code, the result and fields in footnotes, frames,
headers and tables; no change there.

Checked by tests/runtime/check-citations-fieldmarks.py.

